# Developer Experience

## Objective

Optimize for the experience of the next developer (human or AI) working in this codebase: clear errors, good tooling, low friction setup.

## Core Principles

- Error messages should say what went wrong and how to fix it, not just 'error'.
- Keep setup/onboarding (install, run, test commands) simple and documented.
- Provide helpful types/interfaces/docstrings so tooling (autocomplete, type checking) actually helps.
- Avoid 'magic' behavior that isn't discoverable without reading the source.

## Requirements

- When adding a new script/command, document how to run it (README or inline help).
- Write error messages that include the actionable next step, not just the failure.
- Keep public function signatures typed/documented so callers don't have to read the implementation to use them.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Throwing a bare exception with no message on invalid input.
- Adding a new CLI command/script with no documentation on how to invoke it.
- Leaving public functions without types/docstrings, forcing callers to read internals.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ New commands/scripts are documented.
✓ Error messages are actionable, not just descriptive of failure.
✓ Public interfaces have enough type/doc info to be usable without reading internals.

## Golden Rule

Every bit of friction removed for the next developer pays for itself many times over.
